  "question": {
    "text": "In a Young's double slit experiment, the intensity of light at a point on the screen where the path difference between the interfering waves is $\\lambda$, ( $\\lambda$ being the wavelength of light used) is $\\mathrm{I}$. The intensity at a point where the path difference is $\\frac{\\lambda}{4}$ will be (assume two waves have same amplitude)"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "zero",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "I",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "0.5I",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "0.25I",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\mathrm{I}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{I} \\cos ^2 \\phi / 2$\n$\\phi=\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} \\Delta \\mathrm{x}, \\quad \\phi=\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} \\cdot \\frac{\\lambda}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n$\\frac{\\phi}{2}=\\frac{\\lambda}{4}, \\quad \\mathrm{I}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{I}^2 \\cos ^2 \\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\mathrm{I}}{2}$"
  }
}